## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform parallel, lane-specific bit manipulation on a 128-bit value without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Video: decompose 128-bit into four 32-bit words; W0 is lo, W3 is hi
- Per-lane rotation: Ri = (Wi << mi) | (Wi >> ((32 - mi) & 31)); mi = ni & 31
- Reassemble lanes in the same order; handle edge cases with safe shifts

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 rotateWords128(uint128 x, unsigned n0, unsigned n1, unsigned n2, unsigned n3) {
  unsigned m0 = n0 & 31, m1 = n1 & 31, m2 = n2 & 31, m3 = n3 & 31;
  uint32_t w0 = (uint32_t)(x.lo);
  uint32_t w1 = (uint32_t)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32_t w2 = (uint32_t)(x.hi);
  uint32_t w3 = (uint32_t)(x.hi >> 32);
  uint32_t r0 = (w0 << m0) | (w0 >> ((32 - m0) & 31));
  uint32_t r1 = (w1 << m1) | (w1 >> ((32 - m1) & 31));
  uint32_t r2 = (w2 << m2) | (w2 >> ((32 - m2) & 31));
  uint32_t r3 = (w3 << m3) | (w3 >> ((32 - m3) & 31));
  uint128 z;
  z.lo = ((uint64_t)r1 << 32) | r0;
  z.hi = ((uint64_t)r3 << 32) | r2;
  return z;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit lanes in a 256-bit value? 
- What are performance trade-offs vs. a more generic byte-wise permute?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft branchless bit-twiddling in 128-bit space, a common low-level optimization task.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit integer handling via two 64-bit halves
- Trailing-zero count (ctz) tricks without branches
- De Bruijn sequences for fast bit indexing

## Code Example
```c
unsigned countTrailingZeros128(uint128 x) {
  static const unsigned char deBruijn64[64] = {
    0, 1, 2, 53, 3, 7, 54, 27,
    4, 38, 8, 34, 55, 48, 28, 20,
    5, 39, 53, 6, 59, 29, 42, 16,
    9, 35, 60, 50, 45, 27, 21, 17,
    10, 49, 56, 40, 30, 14, 43, 15,
    11, 23, 51, 52, 41, 19, 13, 44,
    12, 26, 58, 36, 31, 37, 29, 22
  };
  unsigned loIdx = ((x.lo & -x.lo) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned hiIdx = ((x.hi & -x.hi) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned tzLo = deBruijn64[loIdx];
  unsigned tzHi = deBruijn64[hiIdx];
  unsigned isLoZero = (x.lo == 0);
  return (1 - isLoZero) * tzLo + isLoZero * (64 + tzHi);
}
```

